{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3001","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3001","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3001\",\"parent_revision\":\"2883e8ae833992893a6b5719d5465a4415dbc63a2cbad592a7893325b3df2ca0\",\"root_revision\":\"2883e8ae833992893a6b5719d5465a4415dbc63a2cbad592a7893325b3df2ca0\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-19\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nmain's CI (Code quality workflow) has been red on every sampled push for as long as history was checked (gh run list --workflow code-quality.yml --event push --limit 15 on 2026-08-18/19 showed 15/15 failure) \u2014 but nothing in this repo currently checks or surfaces that fact on its own. .github/workflows/quality-gate-audit.yml runs every 6 hours and audits whether issues were closed without status:verified, but there is no equivalent scheduled check of whether main itself is currently green. As a direct consequence, a real, live regression (see the companion item on operations.py item_ref contract \u2014 file/link it once created) sat inside that normal red for days, indistinguishable from the job ordinary red-by-default noise, and nobody looked, because there was no signal that specifically said this is new, not the usual red.\\n\\nCandidate direction (not a required approach \u2014 investigate before committing): a small scheduled GitHub Actions workflow (5-15 lines, following the existing pattern in quality-gate-audit.yml) that runs on a cron, checks the conclusion of the most recent completed push-triggered Code quality run on main via gh run list --branch main --limit 1 --json conclusion, and \u2014 if it is failure \u2014 opens or comments on a tracking issue (reusing one persistent issue rather than filing a new one every run, to avoid the same always-red-nobody-looks failure mode this item exists to prevent). This is deliberately the cheapest, smallest thing on this session whole review list \u2014 it does not fix any underlying CI job, it just makes main is currently broken a fact someone gets told instead of a fact someone has to go looking for.\\n\\nThis is a P1: it is cheap (a few lines, following an existing pattern already in the repo), and it would have surfaced the current main breakage immediately instead of it accumulating silently for days \u2014 genuinely higher leverage per unit of effort than most other items from this review.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **feat: ci: no scheduled check surfaces whether main's ci actually went green after each push** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Workflow-quality review, 2026-08-18/19, re-verified live 2026-08-19\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-19\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c24ee0387b8bc73aeecdcb9905ce3b39c33fd0f8c4062ee55175379c3b80da33\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPgvddw\"}"}